div2.p:
I have nourished Literally it runs, I have made them great;
Feci magnos . The term feci (I have made) exhibits the force of the Pihel form, גדלתי(giddalti,) which, as in other instances, approaches the meaning of the Hiphil form, הגדלתי, (higdalti.) — Ed
but as he is speaking about children, we cannot obtain a better rendering than I have nourished, or, I have brought up;
Educavi, vel sustuli.
for instead of the verb, to nourish,
Enutrire.
the Latins employ the phrase, to bring up children
Tollere liberos.
But he afterwards mentions other benefits which he had bestowed on them in rich abundance; as if he had said, that he not only had performed the part of a kind father, by giving them food and the ordinary means of support, but had labored to raise them to an honorable rank. For in every sort of kindness towards them he had, as it were, exhausted himself, as he elsewhere reproaches them,
